  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Origin
    
    The Bra∴conflictu began with an accident. An ancient relic — its nature is not specified in any source I consider reliable — surfaced through deep-ice displacement during routine deep-forge work at ☽'slew Ravium~, a thermal-vent complex in the Nightshelf's lower reaches. Negotiations at Junction Twelve might have resolved the competing claims. They did not. Each faction's precautions foreclosed every alternative, and three armed parties in confined tunnels produced the predictable result.
    
    ## Belligerents and Conduct
    
    The war was three-sided and asymmetrical. The Cell, an owl-sworn faction whose authority was already declining, triggered the conflict and deployed Drift-coalesce — the glacial art that shapes pressure and ice-memory — to weaponize ☽'slew Ravium~ itself. Passages sealed mid-retreat. Thermal chambers were flash-frozen with occupants still inside. The Berg's own memory was corrupted until fresh violence became indistinguishable from ancient trauma in the ice-record.
    
    The ☽'ofund' Guild, a Nightshelf trade faction, fought for the relic. The Wake-Singers, an orca shaman cult devoted to waking their dead god through blood sacrifice, fought for purposes no surviving account adequately explains — though the "Operational Security" chronicle documents Wake-Singer interest in deep-tunnel architecture that predates the conflict. The Nightshelf fought because the war was consuming their home.
    
    Fire-cores burned hotter than anyone had previously dared mine. The fighting produced collapsed shafts, flooded tunnels, and secondary collapses that killed entire family lines.
    
    ## Aftermath
    
    Seven memorial sites hold the names of the dead. The Nightshelf maintains a separate list — names unspoken aloud, belonging to those whose families were entirely lost. The Swaleed~ Luminary, a ceremonial vessel, marks the deepest point where bodies from all three factions were found frozen together; a jagged scar across its volcanic glass is the war's signature damage. The artifact ☽'☽'numu' Witness suffered a clean spinal fracture the night it was carried through the fighting rather than surrendered. No source specifies what the Witness was used for during the conflict.
    
    The war catalyzed the development of Threk's Opening, a pressure-depth combat technique, and is commemorated in at least four Nightshelf legal instruments: Echo∴eliquiae, Foedus∴vincu, Mandatum∴ccord, Memoria∴moria, and Ritus∴um.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771585538339_0", anchor: "at least four Nightshelf legal instruments", text: "Five. I then listed five. The confidence of 'at least' doing no work whatsoever.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771585538339_1", anchor: "fought for purposes no surviving account adequately explains", text: "Too cautious. My own annotation on the ☽'ofund' Guild documents that routes shared at Junction Twelve — during the negotiations this entry describes as failed — map precisely onto the mineral-water channels the Wake-Singers later used to breach The Fissured Smelt~. Their interest was cartographic. They wanted the deep-tunnel architecture because it was a road to sites no surface assault could reach. The theology was real enough, but the operational objective was passage, not relics. I wrote 'no surviving account adequately explains' because the accounts I trust are my own prior annotations, and citing yourself in the main text of your own encyclopedia is a kind of madness I have not yet committed to.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771585538339_2", anchor: "fresh violence became indistinguishable from ancient trauma in the ice-record", text: "This is the sentence I wish I could take back and also the truest one in the entry. ☽'slew Ravium~ is unreadable below stratum 4. Not damaged — unreadable. Drift-coalesce folded the testimony in on itself. I spent eleven days at the site and came away unable to confirm whether impressions I was reading belonged to the Bra∴conflictu, the Faction Wars, or something older that has no name. The Cell did not merely kill people in those tunnels. They killed the record.", type: :commentary, display: :full
